      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wheeling was laid out in 1865 and named after Wheeling, West Virginia, the native home of its first settler.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheeling%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office has been operating in Wheeling since 1866, marking the town’s early communication hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheeling%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first railroad depot opened in 1866 south of the tracks, burned in 1881, and was rebuilt on the north side in 1882.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livcolib.org/History/County/1981/1981wheeling.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the early 1900s, Wheeling’s economy boomed with two brick plants, a short‑lived paper mill, cigar and stave factories, and a tomato canning plant in the 1940s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livcolib.org/History/County/1981/1981wheeling.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before radios and TVs, residents entertained themselves with Old Maid conventions, ice‑cream suppers, chrysanthemum shows, and traveling medicine shows.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livcolib.org/History/County/1981/1981wheeling.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Muddy Creek runs just 1.5 miles west of Wheeling, providing a gentle waterway that once powered local mills.",
